messages:
jordan: Heads-up — customer started batching embeddings and immediately hit errors. They switched from single-call to sending a batch and now get a 400 + a dimension mismatch. Pasting their payload & error they sent.

samir: can you paste the exact request body and the error text? often this is shape-related (single vs batch).

jordan: here's the minimal repro the customer sent: 
```json
POST /v1/embeddings
{
  \"model\": \"rwd-embed-2-large\",
  \"input\": [[\"Exported product description...\"]]
}
```
Response: `400 Bad Request`
```json
{
  \"error\": \"embedding dimension mismatch: expected vector length 1536 but got 1536 for item 0\"
}
```
(They also included SDK call that wraps input twice when converting from their legacy format.)

devon: wait that response text looks odd (expected 1536 but got 1536). Could be an ambiguous error message from the runtime when it sees nested arrays and tries to treat inner array as already-computed embedding.

samir: yep. Most likely double-wrapped input. The API expects either a string or an array of strings as `input`. If you pass `[["text"]]` the server may interpret the inner array as a numeric vector or fail validation.

jordan: customer code (python) looked like: 
```py
# legacy expects list-of-docs, each doc.text is str
docs = [Document(text=t) for t in texts]
body = {
  'model': 'rwd-embed-2-large',
  'input': [ [d.text for d in docs] ]  # <-- accidentally wrapped an extra list
}
client.post('/v1/embeddings', json=body)
```

devon: repro'd locally with that shape — server returns confusing validation and our SDK surface gives them a dimension mismatch downstream when they try to index into embeddings.

engbot: Support ticket created SUP-8247 and tagged eng-runtime.

samir: short-term mitigation: add client-side validation in support code and return clearer message. Example JS validation snippet we can share: 
```js
function normalizeInput(input){
  // Accept string, string[] or string[][] where inner is single batch
  if(typeof input === 'string') return [input];
  if(Array.isArray(input)){
    if(input.length === 0) return [];
    // if first element is an array of strings, assume they accidentally nested once
    if(Array.isArray(input[0])){
      // flatten one level but ensure inner are strings
      if(typeof input[0][0] !== 'string') throw new Error('invalid input inner type');
      return input.flat();
    }
    if(typeof input[0] === 'string') return input;
  }
  throw new Error('input must be string or array of strings');
}
```

devon: we should also update SDKs to validate and emit a helpful 400: `input must be string or array of strings — found nested arrays`. I'll open a quick PR for JS/py clients.

jordan: customer applied the client fix (flattening wrapper) and reports embeddings now return arrays of length 1536 and their downstream cosine index works.

samir: follow-ups: (1) adjust runtime validation message to be explicit, (2) create a small docs note in embeddings section about accepted shapes + examples for single vs batch, (3) add telemetry to catch nested-array requests so we can find other customers hitting this.

devon: filed SDK PRs draft and added tests for single vs batched shapes. Will link once CI passes.

jordan: marking this resolved with workaround. Adding note to SUP-8247: resolved by client-side flattening; eng-runtime to improve error msg. :white_check_mark:
